control.onCreate:
sender.style.overflow = "auto";
sender.innerHTML = `
<style>
.feature-scroll {
  color: #26243c;
  font: 16px/1.55 system-ui, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
  white-space: normal;
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
}
.feature-scroll * { box-sizing: border-box; }
.feature-scroll a { color: #473a93; text-underline-offset: .16em; }
.feature-scroll a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid currentColor; outline-offset: 3px; }
.feature-scroll h1, .feature-scroll h2, .feature-scroll h3,
.feature-scroll p, .feature-scroll figure { margin: 0; }
.feature-scroll header { background: #302a60; color: #fff; }
.feature-scroll .feature-wrap { width: min(100%, 1120px); margin-inline: auto; padding-inline: clamp(16px, 4vw, 40px); }
.feature-scroll header .feature-wrap { padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 48px); }
.feature-scroll h1 { font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 50px); line-height: 1.08; }
.feature-scroll header p { color: #eeebff; margin-top: 10px; }
.feature-scroll .feature-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(205px, 245px) minmax(0, 1fr);
  align-items: start;
  gap: clamp(20px, 4vw, 52px);
  padding-block: 24px 48px;
}
.feature-scroll nav { position: sticky; top: 12px; max-height: calc(100vh - 24px); overflow-y: auto; border: 1px solid #e3dff2; border-radius: 12px; padding: 16px; background: #fff; }
.feature-scroll nav > p { font-weight: 700; }
.feature-scroll nav ul { list-style: none; margin: 6px 0 0; padding: 0; }
.feature-scroll nav ul ul { margin: 3px 0 9px; padding-left: 10px; }
.feature-scroll nav li a { display: block; padding-block: 3px; }
.feature-scroll nav > ul > li > a { font-weight: 650; }
.feature-scroll nav > ul > li > ul a { font-size: 13px; }
.feature-scroll section + section { margin-top: 28px; }
.feature-scroll h2 { font-size: clamp(23px, 4vw, 32px); margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.15; }
.feature-scroll article { background: #fff; padding: clamp(14px, 3vw, 24px); border: 1px solid #e3dff2; border-radius: 12px; margin-top: 10px; }
.feature-scroll article:target, .feature-scroll section:target h2 { outline: 2px solid #7665be; outline-offset: -2px; }
.feature-scroll section, .feature-scroll article { scroll-margin-top: 12px; }
.feature-scroll h3 { font-size: 19px; line-height: 1.25; }
.feature-scroll article p { margin-top: 7px; color: #4e4c63; }
.feature-scroll .feature-example { display: inline-block; margin-top: 9px; }
.feature-scroll article figure { margin-top: 14px; }
.feature-scroll img { display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid #e3dff2; border-radius: 6px; }
.feature-scroll figcaption { color: #4e4c63; font-size: 13px; margin-top: 5px; }
@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .feature-scroll .feature-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .feature-scroll nav { position: static; max-height: none; }
  .feature-scroll nav > ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 190px), 1fr)); gap: 0 14px; }
}
</style>

<div class="feature-scroll" lang="en">
  <header>
    <div class="feature-wrap">
      <h1>ePage Studio features</h1>
      <p>Explore the tools for designing pages, writing application logic, and publishing with eServer.</p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="feature-wrap feature-grid">
    <nav aria-label="Feature index">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ul>
<li><a href="#studio">Design workspace</a><ul>
<li><a href="#main-interface">Main Interface</a></li>
<li><a href="#project-management">Project Management</a></li>
<li><a href="#control-hierarchy">Control Hierarchy Management</a></li>
<li><a href="#control-properties">Rich Control Properties</a></li>
<li><a href="#copy-paste">Copy and Paste</a></li>
<li><a href="#browser-compatibility">Browser Compatibility</a></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href="#layout">Layout and appearance</a><ul>
<li><a href="#page-layers">Page Layer Management</a></li>
<li><a href="#multi-level">Multi-Level System</a></li>
<li><a href="#local-naming">Local Component Naming</a></li>
<li><a href="#visual-layout">Automated Visual Interface Layout System</a></li>
<li><a href="#animation">Built-in Animation Features</a></li>
<li><a href="#themes">Themes</a></li>
<li><a href="#hsi">HSI Transformation</a></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href="#logic">Application logic and data</a><ul>
<li><a href="#control-encapsulation">Reusable Components</a></li>
<li><a href="#global-variables">Global Variables and Class Management</a></li>
<li><a href="#task-set">Task Set</a></li>
<li><a href="#database">Database Support</a></li>
<li><a href="#backend-js">Backend JavaScript Code</a></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href="#server">Publishing and operations</a><ul>
<li><a href="#independent-server">Independent Server</a></li>
<li><a href="#https">HTTPS and TLS 1.3</a></li>
<li><a href="#logging">Server Logging System</a></li>
<li><a href="#request-protection">Request Blocking</a></li>
</ul></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <main>
  <section id="studio" aria-labelledby="studio-title">
    <h2 id="studio-title">Design workspace</h2>
    <article id="main-interface">
      <h3>Main Interface</h3>
      <p>Open a page from the Projects pane, arrange its controls on the design canvas, and edit the selected control in Properties. The toolbar groups layout and editing commands, and the Layers pane shows the page&#x27;s layers.</p>
      <figure><img width="1100" height="560" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="ePage Studio editing the ePage website, with the project tree, canvas, and properties pane" loading="lazy"><figcaption>ePage Studio with the website homepage open on the design canvas.</figcaption></figure>
    </article>
    <article id="project-management">
      <h3>Project Management</h3>
      <p>A solution groups projects in one workspace. Within a project, each page has its own directory and a page.xml file for its controls and scripts. Project.xml lists the pages and resources that belong to the project.</p>
      <figure><img width="650" height="470" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Project tree annotated with solution, projects, and web pages" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The Projects pane organizes pages under projects in the solution.</figcaption></figure>
    </article>
    <article id="control-hierarchy">
      <h3>Control Hierarchy Management</h3>
      <p>Controls are arranged in a parent-child tree. Use the Controls pane to find a component within a nested layout. For example, a button inside a toolbar panel is positioned relative to that panel. Group controls under the parent that should move or resize them together, then inspect the tree when a control appears in the wrong layout or layer.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="control-properties">
      <h3>Rich Control Properties</h3>
      <p>Use Properties to configure text, colors, backgrounds, alignment, sizing, and docking for the selected control. Set shared geometry on a container when multiple child controls need to move together.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="copy-paste">
      <h3>Copy and Paste</h3>
      <p>Copy a control you have already styled to reuse it as a starting point. For repeated navigation items, paste a copy and update its label, name, and click handler for the new destination.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="browser-compatibility">
      <h3>Browser Compatibility</h3>
      <p>eServer publishes pages as HTML and JavaScript. Use the designer&#x27;s screen-size selector to preview narrow layouts, then verify wrapping, scrolling, and interaction in the desktop and mobile browsers you intend to support.</p>

    </article>
  </section>
  <section id="layout" aria-labelledby="layout-title">
    <h2 id="layout-title">Layout and appearance</h2>
    <article id="page-layers">
      <h3>Page Layer Management</h3>
      <p>Put alternate views or transient controls on named layers and switch their visibility within the same page. For example, a selector can reveal a detail panel while the surrounding page stays in place.</p>
      <a class="feature-example" href="./samples#layers">Open the layer demo</a> &middot; <a class="feature-example" href="./tutorials?v=components#build-a-component">Explore a real layered page</a>
    </article>
    <article id="multi-level">
      <h3>Multi-Level System</h3>
      <p>Use nesting to compose several levels of layout and use layers for alternate views at a given level. A page can have a vertical layout with a horizontal toolbar above a content area that contains its own panels.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="local-naming">
      <h3>Local Component Naming</h3>
      <p>A control&#x27;s pathname records its location under its parent. Give controls meaningful names and use their hierarchy to distinguish similar elements, such as labels inside separate cards.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="visual-layout">
      <h3>Automated Visual Interface Layout System</h3>
      <p>Combine horizontal and vertical layouts with docking to let controls follow the size of their container. For example, dock a content panel to fill the space below a toolbar. Decide which areas keep their own height and which expand with the container. Check the result at a narrow width and after resizing the browser.</p>
      <figure><img width="1181" height="99" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="ePage Studio toolbar showing alignment, spacing, and equal-size commands" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The Studio toolbar includes alignment, spacing, and equal-size commands.</figcaption></figure>
    </article>
    <article id="animation">
      <h3>Built-in Animation Features</h3>
      <p>Use motion to communicate a state change, such as revealing a panel. The browser runtime exposes an animation-end callback for actions that need to happen once the transition finishes.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="themes">
      <h3>Themes</h3>
      <p>Apply a shared skin to controls that need a consistent appearance. A skin pairs an image with an XML definition that can describe its content region and which parts of the image may stretch.</p>
      <a class="feature-example" href="./samples#themes">Try the palette preview</a> ? <a class="feature-example" href="./samples?v=admin-controls#admin-controls">Try ProjectAdmin button skins</a>
    </article>
    <article id="hsi">
      <h3>HSI Transformation</h3>
      <p>Hue shifts the color family, saturation adjusts color intensity, and intensity adjusts brightness. These adjustments are useful when adapting the colors of a skin while retaining its existing artwork and edges.</p>

    </article>
  </section>
  <section id="logic" aria-labelledby="logic-title">
    <h2 id="logic-title">Application logic and data</h2>
    <article id="control-encapsulation">
      <h3>Build Reusable Components</h3>
      <p>Start with a basic Div or Panel, add child controls, and define properties and events for the enclosing page. Use <code>onCreate</code> to bind children, initialize per-instance state, and expose methods such as <code>getEditText()</code> and <code>setEditText()</code>.</p>
      <p>Declare an <code>enum</code> in <code>userProperty</code> to add a selectable dropdown to Studio Properties. A <code>jsMix</code> declaration can expose a custom event with its own script editor and named arguments.</p>
      <p>In Faith's desktop main page, the same multiline Edit component appears in Home, a mobile composer, and a dialog. The instances reuse their interface and behavior while setting a different height limit or reacting differently to height changes.</p>
      <a class="feature-example" href="./tutorials?v=components#build-a-component">Learn to build a component</a> &middot; <a class="feature-example" href="./tutorials?v=studio-properties#studio-custom-properties">See the Studio property and event editors</a> &middot; <a class="feature-example" href="./samples?v=live-controls#advanced-edit">Try the advanced editor</a> &middot; <a class="feature-example" href="./samples?v=live-controls#file-upload">Try a component that sends a file to eServer</a>

    </article>
    <article id="global-variables">
      <h3>Global Variables and Class Management</h3>
      <p>Use the page&#x27;s g5 items for values and functions shared between control events. For example, the homepage stores its content frame and the function that loads a page into it. The same JavaScript scope holds the BStyle constructor used to create style objects for multiple navigation controls.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="task-set">
      <h3>Task Set</h3>
      <p>A Task Set keeps a complete browser-to-server operation with the page that uses it. In Studio, give each operation a name such as <code>calculateTotal</code>, <code>getNewsList</code>, or <code>loadNews</code>. Its three scripts live together in the page's <code>page.xml</code>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Script 1 prepares the request in the browser.</strong> Read the current control values and write only the inputs the server needs to the <code>eio</code> buffer.</li>
        <li><strong>Script 2 runs on eServer.</strong> Read and validate those inputs, perform the calculation or service call, and write a reply to <code>eio</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Script 3 handles the reply in the browser.</strong> Read the reply, then update a control, show a message, or populate a table.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Use matching <code>eio</code> types and field order on both sides of each trip. For example, a browser can write two integers with <code>appendInt32</code>, eServer can read both with <code>readInt32</code>, and the reply can use a separate sequence of fields. The page runtime generates the named browser function that sends the request and handles the response.</p>
      <p>Use a Task Set when a button needs server work, such as loading records or calling a service. Keep immediate interface actions, such as switching a visible layer, in the control event.</p>
      <a class="feature-example" href="./tutorials?v=project-admin#task-set-tutorial">Read the Task Set walkthrough and try a live task</a>
    </article>
    <article id="database">
      <h3>Database Support</h3>
      <p>Database connection definitions belong to the project. A list view can send filter values in Script 1, run its query in Script 2, and read the returned rows in Script 3. A separate detail task can request one record by ID when a row is selected.</p>
      <p>The separate ProjectAdmin application uses named tasks for reading paginated results, creating records, and updating task information. Validate input and use parameterized queries for values supplied by visitors.</p>
      <a class="feature-example" href="./tutorials?v=project-admin#project-admin-task-set">See the ProjectAdmin database case study</a> ? <a class="feature-example" href="./tutorials?v=database-guide#database-guide">Follow the database walkthrough</a>

    </article>
    <article id="backend-js">
      <h3>Backend JavaScript Code</h3>
      <p>Backend JavaScript can call a data service, apply business rules, or calculate a value that the browser needs. In the repository's WebService example, Script 2 uses <code>es</code> to request data, then <code>eio</code> to return selected fields. Script 3 reads the data set and updates a table using <code>ee.setTableData</code>.</p>
      <p>Keep service credentials and permission checks on the server. A Task Set can send a short error code for the browser to present when the server cannot complete the operation.</p>

    </article>
  </section>
  <section id="server" aria-labelledby="server-title">
    <h2 id="server-title">Publishing and operations</h2>
    <article id="independent-server">
      <h3>Independent Server</h3>
      <p>Register the project directory in eServer. It reads the page definitions and responds to requests at routes such as /ePage.dev/features. A domain configuration can map its root URL to a project&#x27;s homepage. Save the page definition in the registered project, then open its route and verify the resources and Task Set requests it needs.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="https">
      <h3>HTTPS and TLS 1.3</h3>
      <p>Configure HTTPS and associate a certificate with the site&#x27;s hostname. This site serves HTTPS and has been verified to negotiate TLS 1.3 with the eServer instance on port 443.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="logging">
      <h3>Server Logging System</h3>
      <p>eServer keeps separate logs for server events, network activity, and file handling. When diagnosing a failed page or asset request, use the request time to find the corresponding entries.</p>

    </article>
    <article id="request-protection">
      <h3>Request Blocking</h3>
      <p>eServer installations include a block list configuration. Use request blocking as part of operating a public site, alongside monitoring server logs and applying access controls to your application.</p>

    </article>
  </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</div>`;
